1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,000
A community app for model hobbyists to share photography, organize collections, and
connect with other collectors.

2
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Available on the web and iOS App Store, with a direct Android APK download. Android
is not yet listed on Google Play.

3
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:36,000
The feed brings model photography into a shared community. This walkthrough uses
captured web screens, not a continuous recording.

4
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:48,000
The composer provides a place to prepare a post. The text shown is an unsubmitted
draft; no post is published in this walkthrough.

5
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:00,000
A post detail view keeps the photograph and its surrounding conversation together.
The image shown here comes from the live web interface.

6
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:12,000
Comments let collectors respond to a shared post. The reply shown in the input is an
unsubmitted draft, not a new published comment.

7
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:24,000
The app uses optimistic updates for likes across feed and detail views. The next two
screens explain this pattern in a separate sample.

8
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:36,000
Synthetic demo: the sample updates the like state before a simulated request
completes. A successful response keeps that updated state.

9
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:48,000
Synthetic demo: a rejected unlike request restores the count from 7 to 8 in both
views. This failure is simulated, not triggered in the live app.

10
00:01:48,000 --> 00:02:00,000
I define requirements and user flows, guide AI-assisted implementation, validate
features, track defects, and maintain release documentation.

11
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:12,000
Read the case study and run the code sample to explore the consistency behavior and
its limits. This walkthrough uses web captures; native-device testing is outside its
scope.
